## 💻 Command-Line Traffic Analysis: TShark & Termshark

When working on headless servers without a graphical interface, terminal-based tools are required.

### 1. TShark (Terminal Wireshark)
TShark shares the core engine, syntax, and options of Wireshark, making it ideal for command-line capture and piping outputs to other tools.

#### Essential TShark Switches
| Switch | Command / Description | Result |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **`-D`** | `tshark -D` | Displays available interfaces and exits. |
| **`-L`** | `tshark -L` | Lists supported Link-layer mediums (e.g., ethernet). |
| **`-i`** | `tshark -i eth0` | Specifies the capture interface. |
| **`-f`** | `tshark -f "host x.x.x.x"` | Applies a capture filter in libpcap (BPF) syntax. |
| **`-c`** | `tshark -c 100` | Captures a specific number of packets, then stops. |
| **`-r`** | `tshark -r file.pcap` | Reads from a specified capture file. |
| **`-w`** | `tshark -w output.pcap` | Writes captured traffic to a file (`pcapng` format). |
| **`-P`** | `tshark -P` | Prints packet summaries while writing to disk. |
| **`-x`** | `tshark -x` | Adds Hexadecimal and ASCII representation to the output. |

#### Basic TShark Usage
```bash
# Locate TShark binary
which tshark

# List all capture interfaces
tshark -D

# Capture traffic on 'eth0' and write to a .pcap file
sudo tshark -i eth0 -w /tmp/test.pcap

# Apply a BPF capture filter for a specific host
sudo tshark -i eth0 -f "host 172.16.146.2"
