## 🔍 Capture Filters vs. Display Filters

Filtering traffic correctly is essential, especially when dealing with large packet captures that can consume heavy CPU and RAM resources.

| Feature | Capture Filters 🛑 | Display Filters 🔍 |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Execution** | Set before capture starts. | Applied during or after capture. |
| **Syntax** | Berkeley Packet Filter (BPF) syntax. | Wireshark-proprietary syntax. |
| **Data Impact** | Permanently drops non-matching packets. | Hides non-matching packets without deleting them. |

---

### 🛑 Useful Capture Filters (BPF Syntax)

* `host x.x.x.x` — Captures traffic for a specific host.
* `net x.x.x.x/24` — Captures traffic for a specific subnet.
* `src net x.x.x.x/24` — Captures traffic originating from the target network.
* `port #` — Captures traffic traversing a specific port.
* `not port #` — Excludes traffic on a specific port.
* `portrange x-x` — Captures traffic within a specified port range.

---

### 🔍 Useful Display Filters

* `ip.addr == x.x.x.x` — Filters for traffic involving a specific host IP.
* `ip.src == x.x.x.x` / `ip.dst == x.x.x.x` — Filters by source or destination IP.
* `dns` / `tcp` / `arp` / `http` — Filters traffic by a specific protocol.
* `tcp.port == x` — Filters by a specific TCP port.
* `and` / `or` / `not` — Logical operators to combine multiple conditions.

---

> 💡 **Pro-Tip (Ports vs. Protocols):**
> Filtering by port (`tcp.port == 80`) isolates all data moving across port 80, regardless of the protocol. Filtering by protocol (`http`) instructs Wireshark to scan packet markers (like `GET`/`POST` requests) regardless of which port is used.
